FamilyTree.person({"id":"rene_j","name":"René Janssens","dates":"d. after 2000 Oostende","confidence":"fam","surname":"Janssens","family":"janssens","died":"after 2000 Oostende","branch":"Thumas","line":"janssens","spouses":[{"id":"anna_vc","name":"Anna Van Craenenbroeck"}],"note":"A witloof (Belgian-endive / “wit goud”) farmer — the family grew the famous chicory around Zaventem (Vlaams-Brabant). René and Anna later moved to the coast (Middelkerke), and René died in Oostende (after 2000). His Janssens ancestry is not yet cracked — but NOT because of any hard privacy barrier. Belgium's civil-record publicity rule is graduated (births open at 100 yrs, marriages 75, deaths 50) AND, crucially, a direct descendant may obtain a relative's birth/marriage/death act at any age, so the documents that name René's parents ARE obtainable by the family. The reason online searching hasn't found him is mundane: (a) 'Janssens' is one of Belgium's commonest surnames, so with no anchor (his birth village or year, or a parent's name) his records can't be told apart from thousands of others; and (b) no descendant has published him in an online tree, and his post-2000 coast death notice isn't in the digitised obituary aggregators (inmemoriam, ingedachten, DELA all checked, July 2026). CONTRAST with his wife's side: Anna Van Craenenbroeck's Zaventem family WAS located (see anna_vc) because that surname is rare and village-specific. UNLOCKS for René: his birth village + approx year (enough to request the birth act and pull his parents), a bidprentje, or a relative's memory of his parents' names."});
